# An NDI source answering a browse for _ndi._tcp.local, in the shape that
# used to be thrown away whole.
#
# The point of this fixture is the instance label:
#
#   STAGE-MBP.LOCAL (Scan Converter)
#
# One label, 32 bytes, holding a dot, spaces and parentheses. RFC 6763 §4.1.1
# allows all of it: the service instance name is a single label of arbitrary
# UTF-8, and the dot is text, not a separator. NDI builds the name from the
# machine's hostname, which on a Mac ends ".LOCAL", so every NDI source on a
# Mac looks like this.
#
# golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage rejects any label containing a dot
# (golang/go#56246), and its Unpack is all or nothing, so handing it this
# message failed the lot - the A record below included. shoal decodes names
# itself for exactly this reason; see ParseResponse.
#
#   answer     _ndi._tcp.local                             PTR ttl 120  STAGE-MBP.LOCAL (Scan Converter)._ndi._tcp.local
#   additional STAGE-MBP.LOCAL (Scan Converter)._ndi._tcp.local SRV ttl 120  stage-mbp.local:5961
#   additional STAGE-MBP.LOCAL (Scan Converter)._ndi._tcp.local TXT ttl 120  groups=public
#   additional stage-mbp.local                              A   ttl 120  192.168.1.42
#
# Names are written out in full rather than compressed, which is legal and
# keeps the fixture readable. Class 0x8001 is IN with the mDNS cache-flush
# bit set. Every value here is invented.
